sections.text:
Students often treat planning as description. A plan must propose and justify, not only describe what exists.

Other common errors:
confusing land use with zoning, which is one legal tool for controlling land use;
quoting a law or standard without naming the country and year;
using a density figure without saying whether it is gross or net;
drawing beautiful plans with no evidence on population, cost or demand;
ignoring the people who live in the area, especially the poor;
assuming a plan will be implemented without asking who pays and who has the power to approve it.

Finally, write plainly. A plan that officials and residents cannot understand will not be used.
